<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Trent</h1><p class="st-meta">1027 CE – 1803 CE · capital Trento</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine prince-bishopric on the road from Germany to Italy, best known as the host city of the Council of Trent.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> was a bishopric from the 4th century. In 1027 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Conrad II</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Udalrich II the counties of Trento, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau">Vinschgau</a>, to secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> valley route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The bishops became princes of the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> faced their own advocates, the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, who after 1363 passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and dominated the bishopric through treaties. Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-von-wangen">Frederick Vanga</a> issued an important mining code in 1208. Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-clesio">Bernardo Clesio</a> rebuilt the city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style, and under his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristoforo-madruzzo">Cristoforo Madruzzo</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> met there between 1545 and 1563, launching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Catholic Reformation</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The prince-bishopric was secularised in 1803 and annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-italy-napoleonic">Napoleonic Kingdom of Italy</a> before returning to Austria in 1815. After 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the see remains as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archdiocese-of-trento">Archdiocese of Trento</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-trent.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Trent" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Ketipmaig · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Prince-Bishopric_of_Trent_(1801-1802).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1027 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trento</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Udalrich II, Bernardo Clesio, Cristoforo Madruzzo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Trento, Hochstift Trient, Bishopric of Trento</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1415&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-trent" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Trent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4943 556 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Trent lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5263 812L5229 694M5263 812L5267 775M5263 812L5381 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5267 775h0M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Trento. <a href="/atlas?year=1415&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-trent">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
